<wpf:ResourceDictionary xml:space="preserve" xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml" xmlns:s="clr-namespace:System;assembly=mscorlib" xmlns:ss="urn:shemas-jetbrains-com:settings-storage-xaml" xmlns:wpf="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation">
	<!--
		Solution-shared ReSharper/CLT settings layer. This file sits next to PetBox.slnx and is
		picked up automatically by name (PetBox.slnx.DotSettings) — both `jb inspectcode` /
		`jb cleanupcode` and Rider mount it as a layer, no project reference or config flag needed.

		TWO THINGS THAT COST US A FULL DEBUGGING SESSION, recorded here so nobody re-derives them:

		1. A cleanup PROFILE IS NOT A TREE OF BOOLEAN KEYS. It looks like it should be
		   `/Default/CodeStyle/CodeCleanup/Profiles/=PetBoxSafe/CSOptimizeUsings/OptimizeUsings` with
		   a bool value next to it — that is how most other ReSharper settings are shaped, and it is
		   the wrong shape here. A profile is ONE key, `.../Profiles/=<name>/@EntryIndexedValue`, and
		   its value is an s:String holding an entire escaped XML document (`<Profile>...</Profile>`,
		   HTML-entity-escaped: `<` becomes `&lt;` etc.). Anything written as a nested-key tree is
		   silently ignored — no error, no warning, the profile just behaves as if the setting were
		   never made. The `name="..."` inside the embedded XML and the `=<name>` in the outer key
		   must match byte-for-byte (spaces in a profile name would encode as `_0020` in the key, but
		   "PetBoxSafe" has none).

		2. INSPECTCODE/CLEANUPCODE CACHE SOLUTIONS AND DO NOT INVALIDATE ON SETTINGS CHANGES.
		   CLT keeps a persistent per-solution cache under
		   `%LOCALAPPDATA%\JetBrains\Transient\InspectCode\v262\SolutionCaches\_PetBox.*` (cleanupcode
		   uses the sibling `Transient\CleanupCode` folder). Editing THIS file, or .editorconfig, does
		   NOT bust that cache: a run against a stale cache returns the same findings as before the
		   edit, which looks exactly like "my setting had no effect" even when it did. The only fixes
		   are a `caches-home` option pointed at a folder scoped to the current settings, or deleting
		   the cache folder outright. scripts/inspect-gate.cs derives its `caches-home` value from a
		   hash of this file plus .editorconfig for exactly this reason — see the comment there.

		Both of the above were confirmed with a negative control (profile absent/wrong shape found
		nothing; identical settings behind a freshly scoped cache folder changed the result). Treat
		them as facts, not folklore.

		(NOTE for anyone editing THIS comment block: a literal double hyphen anywhere inside an XML
		comment is illegal and breaks the parse of the WHOLE file silently for these tools — the
		settings loader does not surface an error, it just behaves as if none of the keys below were
		ever set. Spell CLI flag names out in prose without their two leading hyphens together, the
		way this comment block does throughout.)
	-->
	<s:String x:Key="/Default/CodeStyle/CodeCleanup/Profiles/=PetBoxSafe/@EntryIndexedValue">&lt;?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-16"?&gt;&lt;Profile name="PetBoxSafe"&gt;&lt;CSOptimizeUsings&gt;&lt;OptimizeUsings&gt;True&lt;/OptimizeUsings&gt;&lt;EmbraceInRegion&gt;False&lt;/EmbraceInRegion&gt;&lt;RegionName&gt;&lt;/RegionName&gt;&lt;/CSOptimizeUsings&gt;&lt;/Profile&gt;</s:String>
	<!--
		Use it explicitly, never as the implicit default profile (Rider/CLT default to "Full
		Cleanup", which reformats and reorders far more than we want in a gate):

		  jb cleanupcode PetBox.slnx, with the profile option set to PetBoxSafe (see
		  scripts/inspect-gate.cs usage header, or this file's own comments above, for why this
		  comment spells CLI flags out in prose instead of typing their leading hyphens directly)

		Right now this profile does exactly one thing — remove unused usings — on purpose. It is
		the smallest cleanup that is safe to run unattended across the whole solution; anything
		riskier (reformatting, member reordering, "make readonly", etc.) belongs in a reviewed,
		file-scoped pass, not in a profile anyone can invoke solution-wide.
	-->

	<!--
		resharper-clt-line-width (2026-07-29): adding a bare &lt;CSReformatCode&gt;True&lt;/CSReformatCode&gt;
		to this profile was TRIED and REJECTED — recorded here so the next reader does not redo the
		same experiment blind, the same way the CS8602/AccessToDisposedClosure entries above record
		their own negative results.

		The goal was narrow: wrap the ~536 lines over max_line_length (see .editorconfig) that are
		genuinely reformattable code (as opposed to the ~500-600 lines whose length comes from a
		single string literal, which no reformatter touches — see resharper-clt-line-width on the
		work board). CSReformatCode does NOT do that narrowly:

		1. Run through the sanctioned `./build.ps1 -Target CleanupCode` (whole-solution, no file
		   scoping), it rewrote 647 of ~800 tracked .cs files (+13286/-8211 lines) to fix long lines
		   in only ~254 of them — roughly 60% of touched files had ZERO line-width violations and
		   were reformatted anyway (blank-line and brace-placement drift toward R#'s own formatting
		   opinion, not toward this repo's).
		2. That reformatted tree immediately fails FormatVerify's `dotnet format whitespace` with its
		   verify-no-changes option set (the required gate) — R#'s whitespace conventions and
		   dotnet-format's disagree on indentation shape, requiring a SECOND whole-solution
		   `dotnet format whitespace` pass to reconcile, on top of the first oversized diff.
		3. Even scoped down with `jb cleanupcode`'s include option (a hand invocation, NOT
		   `./build.ps1 -Target CleanupCode`, which has no such scoping today), pointed only at the
		   219 files that actually have a code-driven overflow, the tool still rewrites unrelated
		   style in the very files it has a good reason to touch — worked example,
		   src/PetBox.Core/Data/Temporal/TemporalStore.cs: a 41-character
		   `if (named.Contains(key)) rejected.Add(key);` (nowhere
		   near 140 chars) got split onto two lines, and several unrelated blank lines were inserted/
		   removed. Confirmed NOT a regression on the two risks this card's parent flagged by name —
		   target-typed `new(...)` and expression-bodied members both survive reformatting intact in
		   every occurrence checked — but "does not break those two specific things" and "produces a
		   diff a reviewer can actually read" are different bars, and this fails the second one.

		Net: even the single most conservative reformat cleanup item, at either scope, mixes wanted
		fixes (the actual long lines) with unwanted, unrelated stylistic churn in the same commit, and
		the whole-solution scope multiplies that ~2.5x beyond the actual problem. A future attempt at
		the ~536-line code-reformat half of resharper-clt-line-width needs either a different mechanism
		entirely (hand-wrapping, or a smaller/more targeted R# "Arrange" cleanup item that wraps
		argument/parameter lists without also touching brace/blank-line placement — not yet found) or
		build.cs's `CleanupCode` target growing real file-scoping before this is retried solution-wide.
	-->

	<!--
		CS8602 (possible null dereference of `last` in MemoryTools.cs, around the nextCursor
		construction) — SEVERITY OVERRIDE, not a suppression of a single finding.
		`InspectionSeverities/=<rule>` is solution-wide: it changes how ReSharper (Rider included)
		and inspectcode both treat this rule everywhere, which is exactly what we want here, because
		this is not a one-off false positive but a structural disagreement between two analyzers.

		CS-prefixed codes are COMPILER diagnostics, not ReSharper inspections. Directory.Build.props
		sets TreatWarningsAsErrors=true, and inspectcode honors that by reporting CS-codes at ERROR
		severity too — so this same finding is also something `dotnet build` would report, and would
		report first, and more precisely, if it were real.

		Concretely, at MemoryTools.cs (`nextCursor` construction): `last` is read only inside
		`kept.Count > 0 ? ordered[kept.Count - 1] : default`, i.e. exactly the branch where `last`
		was just assigned from `ordered[kept.Count - 1]` rather than left at `default`. Roslyn's
		nullable-flow analysis threads that guard through to the read on the next line; ReSharper's
		solution-wide analysis does not carry the same guard across the two expressions and reports
		a possible-null-dereference that the compiler itself considers proven safe. If this were a
		real bug, `dotnet build` — which actually gates merges — would already be red on it.

		Setting this to DO_NOT_SHOW mutes CS8602 for the ENTIRE solution, not just this call site.
		That is a deliberate, solution-wide trade — accepting that inspectcode will never again be
		the tool that catches a genuine CS8602 — made because the compiler already owns that
		diagnostic end to end. It is not an oversight and not a narrow suppression; if a future
		finding under this rule turns out to be real, `dotnet build` is expected to have already
		failed on it. Do not read this as "we didn't get around to fixing it."

		For a narrower case — accepting N specific existing occurrences of a rule while still
		failing on NEW ones — use the `suppressions` baseline array in scripts/inspect-gate.cs
		instead of widening a rule's severity here.
	-->
	<s:String x:Key="/Default/CodeInspection/Highlighting/InspectionSeverities/=CSharpWarnings_003A_003ACS8602/@EntryIndexedValue">DO_NOT_SHOW</s:String>

	<!--
		RedundantUsingDirective raised from its ReSharper default (WARNING) to ERROR, narrowing the
		gate (threshold stays ERROR, per scripts/inspect-gate.cs) onto exactly this family instead of
		lowering the gate's own severity floor to WARNING, which would light up thousands of findings
		across unrelated rules. This is `resharper-clt-step2-redundant-usings` on the work board: the
		Roslyn/dotnet format stack does not see this class of finding at all (IDE0005 at
		AnalysisMode=All plus TreatWarningsAsErrors=true still reports zero on the same tree where
		inspectcode reports hundreds), so ReSharper is the only tool in this pipeline that catches a
		dead using.
	-->
	<s:String x:Key="/Default/CodeInspection/Highlighting/InspectionSeverities/=RedundantUsingDirective/@EntryIndexedValue">ERROR</s:String>

	<!--
		resharper-clt-step3-defect-shaped: three "possible defect" families raised from their
		ReSharper defaults (PossibleMultipleEnumeration/AccessToDisposedClosure default to WARNING,
		AsyncMethodWithoutAwait/PossibleUnintendedQueryableAsEnumerable default to SUGGESTION/NOTE —
		all below the gate's ERROR threshold) to ERROR, same narrowing shape as RedundantUsingDirective
		above. Every finding these three families produced on 2026-07-29 (5 + 5 + 3) was individually
		read and FIXED at the source (a real async/cancellation bug in MemoryService, plus a handful
		of one-line rewrites — `.ToList()` once, either materializing an ILookup read or before a
		FluentAssertions `.Should()` — that satisfy the analyzer instead of arguing with it). None of
		the three needed a scripts/inspect-gate.cs `suppressions` row: that array is for a finding with
		no such rewrite, not a first resort.

		AccessToDisposedClosure (37 findings, the fourth family in scope) is DELIBERATELY NOT raised
		here — see the standalone note below for why, right after this block.
	-->
	<s:String x:Key="/Default/CodeInspection/Highlighting/InspectionSeverities/=PossibleMultipleEnumeration/@EntryIndexedValue">ERROR</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="/Default/CodeInspection/Highlighting/InspectionSeverities/=AsyncMethodWithoutAwait/@EntryIndexedValue">ERROR</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="/Default/CodeInspection/Highlighting/InspectionSeverities/=PossibleUnintendedQueryableAsEnumerable/@EntryIndexedValue">ERROR</s:String>

	<!--
		AccessToDisposedClosure — SEVERITY OVERRIDE reasserting its own ReSharper default (WARNING),
		not a silent no-op. Written the same way the CS8602 entry above is: a loud, deliberate
		decision, not an omission a future reader should mistake for "didn't get to it".

		resharper-clt-step3-defect-shaped raised this family to ERROR first and read every one of its
		37 findings (2026-07-29, on main c8b918ff) before writing this entry. All 37 turned out to be
		the SAME false-positive shape, in one of two flavors, and NONE was a real defect:

		(a) Production code (20 findings — WorkspaceMembershipService, SqliteFtsIndex,
		    VectorSearchIndex, MemoryService, CommentService, TasksService,
		    CrossScopeTaskSearchService): a lambda captures a `using`-scoped DataConnection/factory
		    delegate to build a nested linq2db query or a short-lived helper (`new SqliteFtsIndex(()
		    => ctx)`), and that lambda is invoked and its result awaited SYNCHRONOUSLY, entirely
		    within the same `using` block, before the connection is disposed. This is not an
		    incidental pattern here — it is the ONLY way AGENTS.md's hard invariant ("No
		    DataConnection is injectable. Ever." / `using var db = factory.Open()`) permits touching
		    the database, and DbInjectionGuardTests fails the build if a connection is ever smuggled
		    out to a longer-lived scope instead. The rule cannot see that the closure never outlives
		    its `using` block; the codebase's own architecture already guarantees it can't.
		(b) Test code (17 findings): the FluentAssertions idiom `var act = () => x.Throw();
		    act.Should().Throw&lt;T&gt;()` (or a `Task.Run`/`Task.WhenAll` branch inside a
		    `using`-scoped harness/semaphore, always awaited before the method returns) — same shape,
		    the closure is invoked and completes before the disposal it supposedly threatens.

		Raising this rule to ERROR under those two shapes would mean suppressing 37 file-level entries
		in scripts/inspect-gate.cs's baseline (that suppression mechanism has no line granularity — see
		the Suppression record there), covering effectively every service in the module and every test
		using the standard assertion idiom, and it would keep re-suppressing the identical shape on any
		future service written per AGENTS.md's own mandated pattern. That is indistinguishable from
		disabling the rule while pretending otherwise, so this codebase treats the rule itself, not any
		one finding, as the wrong unit: severity override, not a suppressions-array entry. If a FUTURE
		AccessToDisposedClosure instance is NOT one of the two shapes above (e.g. a closure genuinely
		escapes its scope via an un-awaited `Task.Run`/fire-and-forget, or a connection is smuggled into
		a longer-lived field — the latter already caught separately by DbInjectionGuardTests), that is a
		real bug this override will hide from the gate; Rider still surfaces it locally at WARNING.
	-->
	<s:String x:Key="/Default/CodeInspection/Highlighting/InspectionSeverities/=AccessToDisposedClosure/@EntryIndexedValue">WARNING</s:String>

	<!--
		NullCoalescingConditionIsAlwaysNotNullAccordingToAPIContract /
		ConditionIsAlwaysTrueOrFalseAccordingToNullableAPIContract — DELIBERATELY NOT RAISED to ERROR.
		Written the same way the CS8602/AccessToDisposedClosure entries above are: a considered
		decision, not an omission.

		resharper-clt-step4-dead-nullable-contract raised both to ERROR first and read every one of
		the 101 findings (79 + 22, on main 17ac944a) they produced. The large majority — traced to
		their actual value source, not judged by expression shape — turned out to be the SAME
		structural mismatch: a C# non-nullable annotation the wire genuinely does not enforce.

		This codebase's MCP tool methods (`[McpServerTool]`), its ASP.NET Core minimal-API JSON body
		DTOs, and its Razor Pages form-bound properties all get their values from the
		ModelContextProtocol SDK's / System.Text.Json's / MVC's own deserializer — none of which
		consults C# nullable-reference annotations at bind time. A required, non-nullable `string`
		parameter/property can still arrive `null` at runtime: an explicit JSON `null` (a malformed or
		adversarial MCP/HTTP client), a JSON array element (`IReadOnlyList&lt;string&gt;` says every
		element is non-null; nothing stops `["a", null]`), or an `init` property whose C# default
		(`= []` / `= string.Empty`) is silently overwritten the moment the JSON names the field
		explicitly as null. Verified two ways, not asserted: (1) traced representative findings to
		their actual origin — MethodologyDefinitionValidator.cs (17 findings) validates a
		user-submitted MethodologyDefinition document BEFORE any of these invariants are established,
		which is the validator's entire job; TasksService/RelationStore/NodeRefResolver/GuardEngine/
		MethodologyRuntime/CommentService/MethodologyInstanceService/TaskSearchFilter/TasksSearchDocs/
		LinkKindsDeclaredMigrator (own MCP-argument boundary); MethodologyWire.ParseDefinition (the
		wire-DTO→domain conversion, sanitizing exactly the array-element-null case); RunSpec.cs
		(deploy JSON persisted to `deploy_deployment.RunSpec`); a dozen HTTP JSON body DTOs
		(ConfigBindingDto, CreateDbRequest, CreateLogRequest, HealthPushRequest, HeartbeatReport, …).
		(2) found existing TEST CODE that proves the gap directly: LogPipelineTests.JsonEnvelope was
		called with a literal `null!` for a declared-non-nullable `serviceKey` (fixed here to `string?`
		— the honest annotation, not a suppression), and MemoryTools.cs's already-documented CS8602
		entry above is the SAME family's compiler-diagnostic sibling.

		A MINORITY were genuinely dead and were fixed at the source instead (removed, not suppressed):
		TsvExporter.RenderCell (raw is provably non-null via its own OR-fallback), LogApi.HasScope(string,string)
		(its one caller already coalesces), LlmRouterApi.ChatCompletionsAsync (a `!` null-forgiving
		operator was manufacturing its own "always true" reading — annotation fixed to `?`, not the
		check removed), plus four test-file simplifications (BoardPageCostTests, MethodologyPresetsTests,
		DefaultHomePreferenceRemovedTests) where the value in THAT specific test is provably non-null
		by construction (a hardcoded preset, a single non-null call site) — none of these touch the
		MCP/HTTP/JSON boundary the two rules mostly misfire on.

		Raising either rule to ERROR would mean growing scripts/inspect-gate.cs's `suppressions` array
		by several dozen file rows for a single shared root cause — exactly the "suppressing a whole
		rule while pretending otherwise" trap the AccessToDisposedClosure entry above already declined.
		Left at the ReSharper default (below the gate's ERROR floor) instead; Rider still surfaces both
		locally. A FUTURE finding under either rule is not pre-judged by this entry — the source of the
		specific value it's arguing about still decides, the same way it did here.
	-->

	<!--
		resharper-clt-step5-dead-public-code, FINAL STEP (severity raise) — NONE OF THE FIVE FAMILIES
		RAISED. Written the same way the CS8602/AccessToDisposedClosure/nullable-contract entries above
		are: a considered decision, not an omission, and not a "didn't get to it".

		The plan when 5a-5e were split off assumed the five families (NotAccessedPositionalProperty
		.Global, MemberCanBePrivate.Global/.Local, UnusedAutoPropertyAccessor.Global/.Local,
		UnusedMember.Global, ClassNeverInstantiated.Global) would reach zero once every zone closed, at
		which point the gate would start guarding them via ERROR severity here. They did NOT reach zero:
		the zones closed with a mix of real fixes, .editorconfig globs (rule x subtree, see the MCP/DTO
		sections above), and documented, deliberate leftovers (xUnit fixtures, DI-hosted services,
		linq2db/reflection materialization). Measured fresh rather than trusting the sub-cards' own
		numbers, which were each taken at a different commit as the effort progressed:

		`jb inspectcode` at severity SUGGESTION (the CLI flag, spelled without its two leading hyphens
		together per the note above), own caches-home option value (NOT the gate's hash, so a stale
		cache can't hide behind this measurement), on origin/main 281e581b (2026-07-29, after all of
		5a/5b/5c/5d/5e and the suppression-mechanism card were merged), exit 0, whole-solution:

		family (repo-wide residual, post-glob):
		  NotAccessedPositionalProperty.Global :  42
		  MemberCanBePrivate.Global            :   5
		  MemberCanBePrivate.Local             :   0
		  UnusedAutoPropertyAccessor.Global    :  32
		  UnusedAutoPropertyAccessor.Local     :  51
		  UnusedMember.Global                  :  62
		  ClassNeverInstantiated.Global        :  11

		Every one of the five families (grouping Global/.Local as one family, matching the parent
		card's own table) has a NONZERO repo-wide residual — including MemberCanBePrivate, where
		.Local alone is 0 but .Global is 5, so the family as a whole does not qualify. Per the parent
		card's own stated rule ("a family with zero residual gets raised, a family with nonzero
		residual does not — a red gate on main is not acceptable"), NONE of the five is raised here.
		This is the valid, unforced outcome of that rule, not a failure to reach it.

		Residual shape, so the next attempt does not re-derive it: MemberCanBePrivate's 5 are new
		since 5c/5d closed (test files added afterward — SqliteDynamicJoinProbeTests.cs,
		UiStateTypeSyncTests.cs — plus one clients-net and one Core/Contract member), i.e. the kind of
		drift a zero-residual family will keep re-accumulating from new code, not a backlog anyone
		missed. ClassNeverInstantiated.Global's 11 are mostly BackgroundService/hosted-service DI
		construction (DeployFailoverSweeper, HealthPoller, KeyStatFlusher, the four *OrphanCleanupService
		classes) plus a couple of DTOs (EntryUsage, Relation) and ShareApi — the same DI-construction
		blindness 5d already named for other hosted services, just not yet swept into a glob or
		annotation. NotAccessedPositionalProperty.Global's 42 concentrate in
		src/PetBox.Web/Mcp/HealthTools.cs (7 — the exact zone 5a's verdict flagged as "outside the
		existing Mcp/Contract/*.cs glob, candidate for its own card, left alone on purpose") plus
		DataDbsApi.cs, StartupMigrationRun.cs and a long tail of one-two-per-file DTOs across
		LlmRouter.Contract, Search, Sessions. UnusedAutoPropertyAccessor's 83 (32 Global + 51 Local)
		lean heavily on materialization shapes already named by 5c/5d (linq2db [Table]/[Column] probe
		rows, KQL/DuckDb dynamic-shape test types) plus LlmRouterApi.cs alone accounting for 15 of the
		51 Local findings. UnusedMember.Global's 62 is the least characterized of the five — the
		largest true backlog, spread thin (Routes.cs 7, AuthConfiguration.cs 4, NodeRefPolicy.cs 4,
		then mostly 1-3 per file across a dozen assemblies) and not yet triaged item-by-item the way
		5b/5d/5e did for their zones.

		To actually reach zero on any one of these needs the SAME zone-by-zone triage 5a-5e already
		did — per-finding review (real dead code removed, confirmed-blind shapes glob'd or annotated),
		not a bulk suppression — repeated against whatever is left. Nothing here blocks that from being
		picked up as new work; it just wasn't this step's job, which was to measure honestly and raise
		only what measurement supports.
	-->
</wpf:ResourceDictionary>
